    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d835">

                <pb facs="1159" n="1031" sameAs="frus:frus1928v01/pg1031"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.542 Symington Co./6</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The General Counsel of The Symington Company</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Mechlin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-03-22"><hi rendition="#italic">March 22,
                        1928</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I wish to advise that the Brazilian
                    situation, brought to your attention by my letter of February 28th, 1928,<anchor
                        n="16" xml:id="d835-f1"/> has been adjusted to our satisfaction in the
                    following manner:</p>
                <p>Familleureux, the Belgian manufacturer, has recognized the validity of the
                    Brazilian patents owned by the Symington Company and has agreed to pay us a
                    royalty for the right to manufacture and import into Brazil Farlow tandem draft
                    gear attachments and journal box lids.</p>
                <p>Permit me to express to you and to the Department the deep appreciation of the
                    officials of the Symington Company for your kind offices in our behalf, without
                    which we would not have been able to carry the matter to its present
                    satisfactory conclusion.</p>
                <p>Yours respectfully,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Ernest F. Mechlin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
